hasizing subnational alignment with national internet governance objectives [Data: Entities (112, 114); Relationships (339)]. This reflects a federated approach where national policy is operationalized through subnational units in Brazil [Data: Relationships (335, 339)].\"\n      },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Cross-border data-flow context highlighted by Alemanha as an endpoint\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The dataset explicitly models cross-border data-flow considerations with ALEMANHA (Germany) as a transnational endpoint in BRASIL–Germany data transfers, illustrating the bilateral data movement dimension within LGPD discussions. The relationship ALEMANHA↔BRASIL confirms data-flow endpoints between the two jurisdictions, signaling regulatory complexity when personal data moves across borders, underscored by the Brazilian framework’s cross-border transfer rules. Germany is further represented as the illustrative endpoint in such flows within diagrams used to explain user-location data transmission, reinforcing the international scope of BRASIL’s data governance narrative [Data: Entities (529, 2); Relationships (515)].\"\n      },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Procedural data-access and record-request framework embedded in the dataset\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The dataset includes explicit procedural elements for records requests and evidence gathering, indicating formal mechanisms to obtain and justify records in civil or criminal contexts. Finalidade do Pedido describes the object of requesting records as forming a probative set for proceedings, signaling a defined legal purpose for data access [Data: Entities (333); Relationships (329)]. Pedido de Registros represents a formal request to a judge to determine custody and provision of records related to connections or internet applications, illustrating procedural steps within BRASIL’s governance context [Data: Entities (330); Relationships (326)]. Requisitos do Pedido outlines the substantive criteria required